# @@PROJECT_NAME_MARKDOWN@@

This repository starts in the production shape expected by GPT Design Bridge. It is
one application with two outputs:

- the production web app: React/Vite in the browser, a typed Hono server, Postmark
  behind the server, and the selected production database;
- the travelling designer drop: a no-build, no-secret, offline-capable browser
  package generated from the same designer-owned JSX and CSS.

External designer participation is optional and begins only when the owner requests a
round. If it happens, the designer is not expected to clone this repository, install
packages, run a build, understand the server, or receive a loose bundle of engineering
code. Engineering ships the runnable drop and later adopts the returned designer
surface wholesale through the recorded bridge procedure.

Accepted designer JSX/CSS is executable interface source, not inspiration. Outbound
screens default to `exact` adoption, which seals returned JSX, CSS, assets, hierarchy,
classes, and visible copy. Production uses React/ReactDOM and Vite; do not introduce
Vue or translate an accepted return into another framework. Brownfield legacy routes
host exact React screens as isolated mounts with adapters outside the sealed UI.

## First run

The repository requires the exact Node 24 LTS line declared in `.nvmrc` and
`package.json`. Do not bypass the engine check.

```text
npm ci --strict-allow-scripts
npm run check
npm run dev:server
npm run dev:web
```

The two development commands run in separate terminals. Copy the selected example
environment file to `.env`; every mode and path is explicit.

## Commands

| Command | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `npm run check` | Runtime, policy, type, unit, integration, and route-parity gates |
| `npm run audit` | Query the registry and fail on a high or critical dependency advisory |
| `npm run build` | Re-run checks, then build browser and server outputs |
| `npm run start` | Start the compiled native server |
| `npm run migrate` | Apply recorded forward migrations to the configured database |
| `npm run database:proof` | Open the configured database and prove representative reads |
| `npm run deploy:doctor` | Fail-loud service/selected-proxy/loopback/origin/public proof |
| `npm run backup -- <release-id>` | Create database-aware backup plus hash evidence |
| `npm run restore:proof -- <evidence>` | Restore into a separate proof target and exercise the app adapter |
| `npm run design:seed` | Deterministically rebuild the travelling SQLite specimen |
| `npm run design:prepare` | Build the no-build stage for the active designer round |

`npm run build` removes stale reproducible `dist/server` immediately before replacing
it; Vite replaces `dist/client`. Other registered artifacts use:

```text
python .gpt-design-bridge/tools/artifact_lifecycle.py report --output evidence/artifact-report.json
python .gpt-design-bridge/tools/artifact_lifecycle.py preview --output evidence/artifact-preview.json
```

Prune requires the unchanged preview hash and explicit `--apply`. Databases, uploads,
secrets, designer returns/escrow, adoption baselines, required evidence, current
release, and sole rollback are never ordinary build output.

The project-local lifecycle command is:

```text
python .gpt-design-bridge/tools/gpt_design_bridge.py status
```

Read `.gpt-design-bridge/contract/ROUND-TRIP.md` before opening a designer round.
Read `.gpt-design-bridge/contract/OWNER-RULES.md` before any work:
`OWNER-RULES-GENERAL.md` applies to every task and `OWNER-RULES-DESIGN.md` applies to
any visible interface or design work, including direct work with no external round.
The exact production database selected for this project is `@@DATABASE_ENGINE@@`.
Read `deploy/README.md` before any host mutation. Generated deployment files are a
procedure, not proof that Hetzner, Cloudflare, the selected Caddy/Nginx proxy,
Postmark, or backups are live.
Read `docs/FALLBACK-LOG.md` first when troubleshooting a possible degraded path; its
stable IDs distinguish registered fallback behavior from a genuine error.
The complete local Lucide/Twemoji asset packs and owner-supplied picker core are under
`public/assets/pickers/icon-picker/`. Read `docs/ICON-PICKER-TEMPLATE.md` before the
first import; the raw source requires one project policy adapter and is not direct-use
UI.
The engineering project keeps those full packs. A designer stage carries every used
asset plus a clearly marked 30-Lucide/30-Twemoji picker sample, measured in
`TRAVELLING-ASSETS.json`; dynamic selections belong in
`designer-assets.json`.
The owner color-picker template is `app/src/ui/ColorPicker.jsx` with
`app/src/styles/color-picker.css`. Read `docs/COLOR-PICKER-TEMPLATE.md` before using
it; the parent owns its `{ hex, opacity }` state and durable persistence.

## Non-negotiable defaults

- Native systemd behind the host's explicitly selected existing Caddy or additive
  Nginx service on a Hetzner VPS; containers are prohibited.
- Cloudflare owns DNS/proxy policy; it does not replace origin hardening or backups.
- Postmark credentials remain server-only.
- The canonical general and design owner rulebooks govern product truth and interface
  behavior; the generated policy checker enforces selected mechanical constraints.
- Browser proof uses
  `.gpt-design-bridge/tools/controlled_chrome.mjs` and its visible pinned Chrome for
  Testing child/fresh profile. The owner's browser/profile and in-app browser do not
  satisfy the release gate. The owned controller proves `file://` directly without
  disabling file security.
